          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            There are various different perspectives on the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %em topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            that looks like a category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+homeomorphisms"} local homeomorphisms
            over a given base
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %em locally modeled on
            a given base space.
          %p
            The archetypical class of examples is that of
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Et
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X) = Et(X)
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+space"} étale space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            that are equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+homeomorphisms"} local homeomorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to X
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = *
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            of all
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            = succeed "." do
              %em modeled on the point
          %p
            What makes the notion of topos powerful is the following fact: even though the general topos contains objects that are considerably different from and possibly considerably richer than plain sets and even richer than étale spaces over a topological space, the general abstract
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
            properties of every topos are essentially the same as those of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            exist, and it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            (even
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            have immediate analogs
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            every topos, and the analogs of the statements about these constructions that are true in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            are true in
            %em every
            topos.
          %p
            On the one hand, this may be thought of as saying that toposes are
            = succeed "," do
              %em very nice categories of spaces
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            %em convenient categories
            for geometry – as in:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+category+of+topological+spaces"} convenient category of topological spaces
          %p
            On the other hand, by de-emphasizing the geometric interpretation of their objects and just using their good abstract properties, this means that toposes are contexts with a powerful
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+order+logic"} higher order logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/law+of+excluded+middle"} law of excluded middle
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            may fail, apart from that, every logical statement not depending on these does hold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            %em every
            topos.
          %p For this reason, toposes are often studied as abstract contexts “in which one can do mathematics”, independently of their interpretation as categories of spaces. These two points of view on toposes, as being about geometry and about logic at the same time, is part of the richness of topos theory.
          %p
            On a third hand, however, we can de-emphasize the role of the objects of the topos and instead treat the topos itself as a “generalized space” (and in particular, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorified
            space). We then consider the topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
            as a representative of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself, while toposes not of this form are “honestly generalized” spaces. This point of view is supported by the fact that the assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\mapsto Sh(X)
            is a full embedding of (sufficiently nice) topological spaces into toposes, and that many topological properties of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            can be detected at the level of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
          %p
            From this point of view, the objects of a topos (regarded as a category) should be thought of instead as
            %em sheaves on
            that topos (regarded as a generalized space). And, just as sheaves on a topological space can be identified with local homeomorphisms over it, such “sheaves on a topos” (i.e., objects of the topos
            %em qua
            category) can be identified with other
            %em toposes
            that sit over the given topos via a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C3%A9tale+geometric+morphism"} local homeomorphism of toposes
          %p
            Finally, mixing this point of view with the second one, we can regard toposes over a given topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            instead as “toposes in the
            = succeed "-world" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %em qua
            categories.
          %h3#what_a_topos_is_like ‘What a topos is like:’
          %p (i) ‘A topos is a category of sheaves on a site’
          %p (ii) ‘A topos is a category with finite limits and power-objects’
          %p (iii) ‘A topos is (the embodiment of) an intuitionistic higher-order theory’
          %p (iv) ‘A topos is (the extensional essence of) a first-order (infinitary) geometric theory’
          %p
            (v) ‘A topos is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+category"} totally cocomplete
            object in the meta-2-category CART of cartesian (i.e. , finitely complete) categories’
          %p (vi) ‘A topos is a generalized space’
          %p (vii) ‘A topos is a semantics for intuitionistic formal systems’
          %p (viii) ‘A topos is a Morita equivalence class of continuous groupoids’
          %p (ix) ‘A topos is the category of maps of a power allegory’
          %p (x) ‘A topos is a category whose canonical indexing over itself is complete and well-powered’
          %p (xi) ‘A topos is the spatial manifestation of a giraud frame’
          %p (xii) ‘A topos is a setting for synthetic differential geometry’
          %p (xiii) ‘A topos is a setting for synthetic domain theory’,
          %p
            And so on. But the important thing about the elephant is that ‘however you approach it, it is still the same animal’.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            The general notion of
            %em topos
            is that of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#ElementaryTopos"}> Elementary toposes
              \.
          %p A specialization of this which is important enough that much of the literature implicitly takes it to be the general definition is the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#SheafToposes"} Grothendieck/sheaf toposes
          %p
            This is the notion relevant for applications in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+logic"} geometric logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
          %p
            For standard notions of mathematics to be available
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} inside
            a given topos, one typically at least needs a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#WToposes"}> W-topos
              \.
          %h3#ElementaryTopos Elementary toposes
          %p
            A quick formal definition is that an
            %strong elementary topos
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            which
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                has
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finite limits
            %li
              %p
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            %li
              %p
                has a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
          %p There are alternative ways to state the definition; for instance,
          %ol
            %li
              has
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite limit
            %li
              has
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+objects"} power objects
          %p In a way, however, these concise definitions can be misleading, because a topos has a great deal of other structure which plays a very important role but just happens to follow automatically from these basic axioms. Most importantly, an elementary topos is all of the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"}> locally cartesian closed
              ,
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+cocomplete+category"}> finitely cocomplete
              ,
            %li
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %li
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
          %p
            The last two imply that it has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            that resembles ordinary mathematical reasoning, and the presence of exponentials and power objects means that this logic is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+order+logic"} higher order
          %h3#SheafToposes Grothendieck/sheaf toposes
          %p
            The above is the definition of an
            %em elementary
            topos. We also have the (historically earlier) notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %strong Grothendieck topos
            is a topos that is neither too small nor too large, in that it is:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocomplete+category"} cocomplete
                (not too small), and
            %li
              %p
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generating+set"} generating set
                (not too large).
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone02"}> Johnstone 2002, Vol 1 Theorem 2.2.8 (iv)
            )
          %p
            Equivalently, a Grothendieck topos is any category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
            on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          %h3#WToposes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            \-toposes
          %p
            There is a further elementary property of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            that might have gone into the definition of elementary topos, but historically did not: the existence of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            %strong topos with NNO
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                \-topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-types"} W-types
          %h3#toposes_over_a_base Toposes over a base
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
          %h3#morphisms_of_toposes Morphisms of toposes
          %p
            There are two kinds of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            between toposes that one considers:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"}> geometric morphism
                – this is the kind of morphism that regards a topos as a generalized
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+morphism"}> logical morphism
                – this is the kind of morphism that regards a topos in terms of its
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
          %p
            Accordingly there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Topos"} Topos
            of toposes, whose
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"}> object
                s are toposes,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"}> morphism
                s are
                = succeed "s," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-morphism"}> 2-morphism
                s are
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#extensivity Extensivity
          %p
            Every topos is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+category"} extensive category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes+are+extensive"} toposes are extensive
          %h3#adhesiveness Adhesiveness
          %p
            Every topos is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adhesive+category"} adhesive category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            this follows immediately from the adhesion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LackSobocinski"} Lack-Sobocinski
          %h3#epimorphisms Epimorphisms
          %p
            In a topos,
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/epimorphism"} epimorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28epi%2C+mono%29+factorization+system"} (epi, mono) factorization system
            in a topos is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+factorization+system"} stable factorization system
          %h3#relation_to_abelian_categories Relation to abelian categories
          %p
            While crucially different from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AT+category"} AT category
          %h3#reasoning_in_a_topos Reasoning in a topos
          %p
            Any result in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+mathematics"} ordinary mathematics
            whose proof is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+mathematics"} finitist
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            automatically holds in any topos. If one removes the restriction that the proof be finitist, then the result holds in any topos with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boolean+topos"} boolean topos
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pi-pretopos"} pretopos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+pretopos"} Heyting pretopos
          %p
            Therefore, one can prove results in toposes and similar categories by reasoning, not about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            in the topos themselves, but instead about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            in the normal language of structural
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal language
            of a topos is called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mitchell-B%C3%A9nabou+language"} Mitchell-Bénabou language
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#specific_examples_and_key_results Specific examples and key results
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The archetypical topos is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
                on the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
                is also an elementary topos, and the inclusion functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinSet
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet \hookrightarrow Set
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+morphism"} logical morphism
              %p
                More generally, for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal"} strong limit cardinal
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_\kappa
                of sets of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinality"} cardinality
                less than
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                is a topos.
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                any (small)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} category of sheaves
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Sh
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(C)
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud%27s+theorem"} Giraud's theorem
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    The case where the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
                    is the trivial one, so that also all categories of
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaves
                    (on small categories) are (Grothendieck) toposes.
                %li
                  %p
                    The case of sheaves on (the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                    given by the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
                    of) a
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
                %li
                  %p
                    The category
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi G
                          %mi Set
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G Set
                    of
                    = succeed "s" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                    of a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                    of
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %li
              %p
                Continuing the previous example, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is a topological group, then the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G Set
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+G-sets"} sets with a continuous action of G
                (that is, the action map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo ×
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\times X\to X
                is
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                has the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            %li
              %p
                More generally,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                may be a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoid"} topological groupoid
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+groupoid"} localic groupoid
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is again a topological group, then the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Unif
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Unif(G)
                of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniformly+continuous+map"} uniformly continuous
                = succeed "-sets" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+completion"} profinite completion
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                = succeed "-set" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed "-set" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                = succeed "-set" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %li
              %p
                For
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                a topos and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in E
                any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overcategory"} overcategory
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E/X
                is also a topos. (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"}> Elephant
                , A.2.3.2). See also
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/over-topos"} over-topos
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                is a topos and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi E
                      %mo →
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j \colon E \to E
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} lex
                idempotent monad, then the category of
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                corresponds to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+topology"} Lawvere-Tierney topology
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+negation"} double-negation topology
            %li
              %p
                An obvious example of an elementary topos that is not a Grothendieck topos is the category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
                of finite sets. More generally, for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+limit+cardinal"} strong limit cardinal
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi κ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
                is an elementary topos, and as long as
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi κ
                        %mo &gt;
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa \gt \omega
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NNO"} NNO
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mo &lt;
                          %mi κ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set_{\lt \kappa}
                does not even admit a geometric morphism to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %li
              %p
                Since the definition of elementary topos is “algebraic,” there exist
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+topos"} free topos
                = succeed "s)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+functor"} logical functor
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monadic"} 2-monadic
                over the 2-category of categories, functors, and natural isomorphisms. It has an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
                = succeed "." do
                  %em the free topos
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
                (of the sort which can be interpreted in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
                of a topos) generates a free topos containing a model of that theory. Such toposes (for a consistent theory) are never Grothendieck’s.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
                with a small set of objects, then the category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi G
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [G,Set]
                (which makes sense if we define “large” and “small” relative to a
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
                and cocomplete, but it does not have a small generating set, and so is an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unbounded+topos"} unbounded topos
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℱ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+objects"} subterminal objects
                in any topos
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %span.newWikiWord
                  filterquotient
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/filterquotient"} ?
                topos
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi ℱ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E//\mathcal{F}
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                are toposes and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon C\to D
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+functor"} lex functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Gl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gl(F)
                called the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
                of
